From 1d6e036bd29b7ba78823490e5785c8d517f698f2 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 7 Nov 2026 14:39:42 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] Visible weather under the new look: grey skies, lit rain, wet
 ground and lightning

Elwynn's weather now shows as WoW Forever's does. The server sends 1.12's
own weather; the new look reads the same state and dresses it.

Overcast: the painted sky's cover closes over grey, the sun's light dims
and cools, the god rays and the grade's warmth fade, and the sun shadows
soften to a grey day's. The mist and the wind already followed it.

Rain: 1.12's drops are drawn as streaks lit by the hour's light and the
torches near them, leaning downwind. Rings spread on the water near the
camera. The ground, the cobbles, the stone and the roofs darken in soft
patches over a minute of rain and take a wet sheen: the sun where it
reaches, the sky at a grazing angle, and every torch pooling on the wet
road at night. They dry over about five minutes after.

Storm: heavier rain, gustier wind, and about every half minute a brief
cool double flash that lights the world and blooms, with thunder a few
seconds after if a thunder sound is found in 1.12's sound table. The
strikes run from the weather packet now showing (its arrival, seeded by
the server's day and its grade), so every hero who heard it sees the same
flashes; the server's storm cue keeps them from repeating. Not the game
clock, which Elwynn pins at 23:30 and sets back every five minutes.

worldWeather 0 turns all of it off on its own; lookweather <clear|
overcast|rain|storm> [0-1] holds a weather here for film through 1.12's
own path, and lookweather server lets go (back to the last weather the
server sent). worldLook 0 is 1.12's weather exactly.

Revised after the first weather films (#271, take 272):

- Night rain at Goldshire filmed near-black and murky, darker and flatter
  than the dry frame. By night an overcast takes 0.15 of the moon's light
  (by day 0.5 of the sun's), soaked ground darkens by 0.1 (by day 0.3), and
  the weather lifts the mist to 0.4 of its daytime lift.
- The wet ground mirrors the lights: every new-look light (a lamp, a torch,
  a building's window light) lies on wet stone as a streak from under it
  toward the camera, out to two and a half times its radius, brightest in
  the puddles, where the old sheen was a small highlight inside the light's
  own pool.
- The streaks catch the lamps: 2.0 of a lamp's light, was 0.8, out to 1.6
  times its range.
- The thunder played kit 8432 (ThunderaanAttack, a boss's attack). It is
  chosen now only from kits whose every file sits under the world's ambience
  or weather sounds, never a creature's, a spell's, a character's or an
  item's, nor a name of an attack, wound, death, aggro, cast or impact; the
  pick is logged, and with none the storm is silent.

